// KNOWN FAILURE: a closure captured by `spawn` and called concurrently from
// multiple OS threads is a genuine DATA RACE with lost updates -- a silent
// wrong answer under concurrency, not a crash. Reproduces on BOTH backends
// (10/10 on Cranelift/JIT, 7/10 on LLVM/AOT at this scale -- probabilistic,
// run it many times).
//
// Root cause: `Instruction::Spawn`'s arg-copy match
// (kryos-codegen-{cranelift,llvm}/src/codegen.rs) treats every OTHER heap
// capture kind (str/array/map/struct/enum) as a value to SNAPSHOT -- a fresh
// deep copy the spawned thread privately owns, matching docs/09-concurrency.
// md's documented contract ("every capture is a snapshot ... this holds
// uniformly for arrays, structs, maps, and strings"). The `MirType::
// Function` / `MirType::Shared` arm is the ONE exception: it calls
// `kryos_arc_retain` on the closure's env box instead of cloning it, so
// EVERY spawned thread that captures the SAME closure value shares the
// exact same env allocation.
//
// That would be merely "closures don't snapshot" (a documentation gap) if
// the shared env were read-only. It becomes a genuine race because a
// closure with a single mutated scalar capture whose tail value IS that
// identifier (CLAUDE.md gotcha #11's "RESOLVED" persistence case) persists
// state via a NON-ATOMIC read-modify-write: the generated `{name}_env`
// thunk calls the closure body (which reads env[slot], computes, and
// returns the new value) and THEN, as a separate instruction, `store`s the
// return value back into env[slot] -- see the thunk codegen in both
// backends, `if let Some(slot) = mutated_slot { ... store ... }`. Two
// threads calling the same closure concurrently can both read the same
// env[slot] value before either writes back, so one thread's increment is
// silently lost. This is the identical bug CLASS as ledger item 7 (mutated-
// scalar-capture persistence never got a real addressable slot), but a
// DISTINCT manifestation: item 7 is about single-threaded repeated calls
// losing generalization past N=1 captures; this is about the ONE persistence
// case item 7 calls "RESOLVED" turning into a cross-thread race the moment
// the closure is shared via `spawn` instead of called from one thread.
//
// Not fixed here (concurrency wave, scope discipline): the honest fix is
// either (a) make Function/Shared spawn captures snapshot like every other
// heap kind (requires a per-closure-shape "deep copy this env" codegen
// helper, generated per closure the same way `__kryos_drop_<Struct>` is
// generated per struct -- nontrivial, both backends), or (b) make the
// call+writeback atomic under a lock (defeats the purpose of an
// uncontended fast path for the common single-thread case). Either is a
// representation/codegen change comparable in size to the already-declined
// item 3/item 7 fixes, not a one-line patch. Filed per this session's scope
// discipline: fix what's found, or leave an honest, reproduced, root-caused
// note for whoever picks it up next.
//
//     kryos run tests/known_failures/spawn_closure_shared_env_race.kry        # Cranelift, run several times
//     kryos build --release tests/known_failures/spawn_closure_shared_env_race.kry -o /tmp/x && for i in $(seq 10); do /tmp/x; done

use std::sync::{wait_group}

fn main() {
    let mut count: i64 = 0
    let bump = || {
        count = count + 1
        count
    }

    let wg = wait_group()
    let threads = 50
    let per_thread = 2000
    wg.add(threads)
    let mut i = 0
    while i < threads {
        spawn {
            let mut j = 0
            while j < per_thread {
                bump()
                j = j + 1
            }
            wg.done()
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    wg.wait()

    // Read the CLOSURE's own persisted state via its own return value, not
    // the outer `count` (which is separately, and correctly per gotcha #11,
    // documented to stay frozen at 0 -- mutated captures are by-move).
    let final_val = bump()
    let expected = threads * per_thread + 1
    println("final={final_val} expected={expected}")
    if final_val == expected {
        println("RACE-FREE (this run got lucky -- rerun; see file header for observed failure rates)")
    } else {
        println("RACE DETECTED: lost update(s), got {final_val} want {expected}")
    }
}
